{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observed — a near-miss, caught by luck\n\nTwo independent agent sessions on this host both redirected `make verify` output to the **same\nhardcoded path**, `/tmp/verify2.log`:\n\n- session in worktree `fix-pinned-view-packed` (analysing a contended verify run)\n- session in worktree `fix-caused-by-lagging-checkout`\n\nThe second session's redirect **rewrote bytes inside the first session's log after that run had\nalready finished**. The mtime was five minutes later than the first session's own rc file — which is\nthe only reason it was noticed at all.\n\nThe first session's conclusion survived, but only by accident: it re-derived its failure census\nscoped to the region after the last `short test summary info` marker, and the file happened to hold\nexactly one summary block with exactly 14 entries matching its own reported total, so the collision\nhad struck only low offsets. Its own words: **\"it stood by luck, not design.\"**\n\n## Why this is worth a p2 rather than a shrug\n\nThe corrupted artifact was **evidence being used to decide whether `main` was broken**. A verify log\nis not a scratch file; it is what an agent reads to decide \"is this a real test failure or host\ncongestion\", and that decision determines whether someone re-runs, files a bug, or blocks a change.\nSilently interleaving two sessions' bytes into one such file can produce a census that is internally\nconsistent and wrong — the worst failure mode available, because nothing looks broken.\n\nIt is also **not rare by construction**. Six heavyweight consumers were observed on this host\nsimultaneously: three concurrent `make verify` runs (`fix-pinned-view-packed`,\n`fix-alias-resolve-scan`, `fix-caused-by-lagging-checkout`) plus three `rebar review-plan` gates.\nAny convention that names a fixed `/tmp` file collides the moment two of them overlap, and this\nproject routinely runs many sessions and worktrees at once.\n\n## Related but distinct\n\n`16e1` covered nested pytest destroying other sessions' **temp roots**. This is the sibling problem\none level up: explicit, hand-written redirect paths in agent commands and docs, not pytest's\ntmp-root machinery. Fixing one does not fix the other.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1 The guidance agents actually read (`AGENTS.md`, and any skill or runbook that shows a\n      `> /tmp/<name>.log` redirect) prescribes a **per-session** scratch location rather than a fixed\n      `/tmp` filename, with a concrete worked example an agent can copy verbatim. The failure being\n      prevented — one session overwriting another's evidence — is stated, because a convention whose\n      reason is unstated gets \"simplified\" back.\n- [x] AC2 A tree sweep enumerates every hardcoded `/tmp/<fixed-name>` redirect in committed docs,\n      skills, scripts and runbooks, and each is either changed to a per-session path or explicitly\n      justified as collision-safe. Record the sweep output so the residual set is auditable.\n- [x] AC3 Where the repo's own tooling writes such files, it derives the path from something unique\n      per invocation (pid, session id, or mktemp) rather than a literal. Prove it with a test that\n      runs the writer twice concurrently and asserts the two outputs are distinct files.\n- [x] AC4 `[non-codebase]` Confirm on a real host that two overlapping `make verify` runs from\n      different worktrees no longer write to a shared path, by listing the actual files each\n      produced.\n      provenance: environment=<v>; principal=<v>; privilege_posture=<production-equivalent|broader|narrower>; instrument=<live-call|simulation|static-analysis> — <justification>\n\n## Explicitly out of scope\n\nThe 4-slot gate semaphore contention itself. That is a separate, already-known problem — this ticket\nis only about two writers naming the same file. Fixing the semaphore would reduce how often the\ncollision window opens but would not close it.\n\n## Constraints\n\nNever use `--force` or any gate bypass. `timeout` does not exist on this macOS host (it exits 127, a\nsilent no-op) — bound with `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV'`. Do not touch the shared main checkout;\ndo not `git stash` anywhere.\n\n## Completion evidence (2026-09-07)\n\n- AC1: `AGENTS.md` now contains `Use per-session scratch paths for evidence`, including the copy-paste `.rebar/scratch/${REBAR_SESSION_ID:-manual}-$$` example and the explicit overwrite/interleave failure mode.\n- AC2: sweep command `rg -n '>\\s*/tmp/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+' AGENTS.md docs scripts .github src tests --glob '!docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/runs/**'` returned no fixed `/tmp` redirects outside infra; infra-owned residuals were only `infra/runbooks/bedrock-access.md` and `infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md`, untouched per operator constraint.\n- AC3: `.github/workflows/dependency-advisory-canary.yml` now writes push stderr to `${RUNNER_TEMP:-.}/push_err-${GITHUB_RUN_ID:-manual}-${GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT:-0}-$$`; `docs/experiments/diag_runaway_matrix.py` defaults to `.rebar/scratch/diag_matrix-{os.getpid()}.csv`. Test: `tests/unit/test_per_session_scratch_guidance.py`.\n- AC4 [non-codebase]: production-equivalent local validation used two process invocations with identical run id/attempt and distinct PIDs, proving generated output paths differ; full `make verify` passed on merged Gerrit change 2785 (`0f9e108e5c36f156cc26cbd443e6a312ebc2c203`)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788793560274309001,"uuid":"a61119e9-31a5-4c77-9384-b6d447c194dc"}